+## Bytes, limits and open exits
+
+Every expected stdout is finite ASCII JSON: recursively lexical object keys, two-space indentation, standard JSON quoting/separators, array order preserved and exactly one terminal LF. Independent plain Python JSON serialization of chosen literal data supplies these bytes, using the same explicitly frozen finite codec as the earlier normal Core/1 packet. There are no float/non-ASCII/escape ambiguities. This is an exact consumer expectation separate from semantic JSON equality, not a general serializer theorem or an actual exporter observation. Expected stderr is an empty byte file. Standalone Program/Execution pretty files do not define embedded subtree byte slices or runtime canonical identities. Whole stdout bytes are under1MiB and remain subject to a future real consumer check. A mismatch must remain evidence; do not regenerate goldens from actual output or weaken equality.
+
+All selected expected JSON fields, ExprIds, values, prefixes and bytes are concrete under the disclosed public-design/representation/encoding premises; no placeholder remains in these six expected envelopes or two Programs. Actual parser admission, full Program correspondence, ordinary/metered output, exit/I/O behavior and full byte compatibility remain **UNRUN**. These fields' earlier unresolved status stays historical. Compiler/circuit/ABI/program identity algorithms outside this contract, authenticated history/time/funding, native/proof/ledger behavior and external/DUST/service/wall-time costs are unresolved or unmeasured, never zero by implication.
